Description: Copy the submodule name for %s/%S using its own length
 The %s and %S cases of modfile_name() loop while modulename[j] is non-zero but
 copy submodulename[j].  For a submodule the module name is the parent's, so a
 parent name longer than the submodule name reads past the submodule name, and
 a shorter one truncates it.  Reproduced with AddressSanitizer:
 heap-buffer-overflow read at modfile_name.c.
 .
 Only formats using %s or %S are affected; the default submodule format "%f.o"
 is not.  For those formats -S output changes where the name was truncated or
 overrun: a submodule mdf_s01 of module mdf gave mdf@mdf.smod for "%m@%s.smod",
 and now gives mdf@mdf_s01.smod.
Author: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1155614
Forwarded: https://bugs.debian.org/1155614
Last-Updated: 2026-11-28
--- a/modfile_name.c
+++ b/modfile_name.c
@@ -74,7 +74,7 @@ char *modfile_name (const char *modulename,
 		    if (!submodulename) {
 		      warning("Submodule component not defined in this context");
 		    } else  {
-		      for (j = 0; modulename[j]; j++)
+		      for (j = 0; submodulename[j]; j++)
                         modfile[mi++] = tolower (submodulename[j]);
 		    }
 		    break;
@@ -82,7 +82,7 @@ char *modfile_name (const char *modulename,
 		    if (!submodulename) {
 		      warning("Submodule component not defined in this context");
 		    } else  {
-		      for (j = 0; modulename[j]; j++)
+		      for (j = 0; submodulename[j]; j++)
                         modfile[mi++] = toupper (submodulename[j]);
 		    }
                     break;
